# NOTES
# 1. Structural pattern: the heuristic guesser appears to have propagated "Skoda" as turbine_supplier for EVERY VVER project uniformly (Rooppur, Belarusian, El Dabaa, Bushehr, Akkuyu). This is confirmed WRONG for Bushehr 1 (LMZ/Electrosila) and Akkuyu 1-4 (GE Steam Power/Arabelle), and is suspected wrong for the rest (Power Machines/LMZ-Electrosila is the standard Russian VVER turbine supplier; Skoda JS is a reactor-internals/component maker, not a turbine house) — but Rooppur, El Dabaa, and Bushehr-2 could not be individually confirmed or refuted, so they are marked UNVERIFIABLE rather than WRONG. Recommend a follow-up sourcing pass specifically on Power Machines' project list.
# 2. Similarly, "Izhora Plants" was propagated uniformly as rpv_manufacturer across all VVER-1200 projects (Rooppur, Belarusian, El Dabaa, Akkuyu). Evidence shows this is NOT uniform: Belarusian's vessel was confirmed built by Atommash/Volgodonsk (AEM-Technologies), while Akkuyu unit 2's vessel was confirmed built by Izhora Plants. Modern VVER-1200 RPV production appears to be split across both Russian forges depending on project/order book, so each project's attribution should be verified individually rather than assumed constant.
# 3. Laguna Verde (Mexico) had two independently-confirmed WRONG fields: architect_engineer was recorded as "GE" but the actual AE firms were Burns and Roe (initial) and Ebasco Services (design/supervision); turbine_supplier was recorded as "General Electric" but Wikipedia directly states Mitsubishi Electric manufactured the steam turbine. GE's role was reactor (NSSS) vendor only.
# 4. Angra 1 (Brazil) and Embalse (Argentina) both show the common heuristic error of collapsing the NSSS vendor's name into the architect_engineer / turbine_supplier fields when a different firm actually held that role (Gibbs and Hill/PROMON for Angra 1's AE; Italimpianti for Embalse's turbine/conventional-island supplier).
# 5. Bushehr is a genuine hybrid supply chain as flagged in the task brief: unit 1 combines reused 1970s-80s German (KWU/Siemens) equipment with a Russian (Atomstroyexport) VVER-1000 core and Russian turbine (LMZ/Electrosila) — the dataset's pure-Russian attribution for turbine_supplier is confirmed wrong for unit 1.
# 6. CAREM25 (Argentina): CNEA is confirmed as the reactor's designer, but CNEA is a state research/regulatory body, not an industrial manufacturer — its listing as turbine_supplier and rpv_manufacturer could not be confirmed and is flagged as a likely category error (design authority vs. component fabricator) rather than blessed as correct.
# 7. Several "constructor"/"architect_engineer" CONFIRMED-MED verdicts (Atucha, Angra 2/3, El Dabaa, Koeberg AE, Barakah AE) rest on the vertically-integrated-turnkey-vendor pattern common to these single-source NSSS programs rather than an explicit source naming a distinct AE firm; flagged MED rather than HIGH confidence for that reason.
